# Build context hygiene (P19-T01).
#
# The context is what `docker build` sends to the daemon, and anything in it can
# end up in a layer. The Dockerfile copies explicit paths rather than the tree,
# so this file is the second line of defence, not the first: it keeps the
# context small and keeps a file that must never leave the machine — a local
# `.env`, the Git history, the local plan — from being available to a `COPY`
# someone adds later.
#
# `tools/imageaudit` requires this file to exclude the paths below: an image
# that lost one of them would still build, and that is exactly why the gate has
# to name them.

# Version control and the local execution plan (never published, never built).
.git
.gitignore
.github
.local

# Environment files and secrets. `.env.example` is a template of variable names
# and safe examples, but it carries no value any image needs, so the whole
# family is out together.
.env
.env.*

# Local tooling and documentation that no stage reads.
.editorconfig
.gitmessage
docs
tests
tools/e2e
scripts
compose.yaml

# Dependencies: reinstalled inside each stage from the lockfiles, which is what
# makes the build reproducible instead of dependent on this machine.
node_modules
**/node_modules

# Build artifacts: each stage produces its own, and a stale `web/dist` copied
# into the image would be served in place of the build that was just made.
web/dist
web/generated
web/test-build
bin
dist
coverage.out

# Editor, OS and temporary files.
.idea
.vscode
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db
tmp
*.log
